MOMENTA PUSHES ENVELOPE, WRESTLES MUD

by Ray Valdes
(c) Copyright 1991 by Ray Valdes. All Rights Reserved.

During the product launch event on Monday October 7th,
Momenta's Kamran Elahian mentioned a number of times how
his company was "pushing the envelope" of software
and hardware technology in creating the machine.

The carefully orchestrated event certainly pushed the envelope
of public relations technology.  But also seems that Momenta was
pushing the limits of truthfulness in some of its claims made
during that launch. 

The grand finale to the 2-1/2 hour event occurred when Elahian
announced that "we are shipping", as the curtain arose on a stacked
mountain of Momenta packages, presumably containing customer-ready
computers.

Yet, on the following day at the OOPSLA '91 conference in Phoenix,
many of Momenta's technical staff, all of whom had been at the
product launch, had a different story to tell. One person said that
the Momenta software was in alpha stage now, would be go to beta
testing in a month or so, and be ready within a month or two after
that.  Another person said that they will ship by the first of the
year.  A third person said they would ship in about a month.  No one
said they were shipping now.  Back in Mountain View headquarters,
one of Momenta's technical evangelists said they would ship
by December 1st.

Certainly the software on the Momenta systems being demonstrated
at OOPSLA was of alpha quality.  The system was oftentimes slow
and unresponsive.  Selecting an graphical object on the screen,
such as a circle or piece of text (on a page that had less than
a handful of objects created) by tapping on it took as much as a
second, rather than the instantaneous response that users of
Macintosh and Windows expect in such a situation.  Often the tap
(equivalent to a mouse click) would be ignored and would have to
be repeated.  Hooking up a keyboard to the system, and typing
one sentence in the word processing program, it took
approximately *five* seconds for the system to display the
sentence after it had been typed (I am a very fast typist,
however).  

During one technical seminar at the conference, a Momenta
staffer stated that the software needed at least a factor of two
improvement in speed before it would be usable by customers. 

While the system did not crash during repeated demos at OOPSLA
(a tribute to the robustness of the underlying Digitalk
implementation of Smalltalk), occasionally it would trap system
errors and display them in a dialog box on the screen.  Also,
the staff demonstrating the system cautioned me against trying
certain operations for fear the system would malfunction. 

There were two technical presentations scheduled to describe
the development environment, which requires a desktop based system.
However, Momenta staff were unable to demonstrate the system
because of problems with the desktop machine.  A second machine
was flown out for the presentation scheduled on the following
day, and Momenta staffers were up late into the night to
make it work.  But the second system would also not run
the Momenta development environment, due to problems with
the Smalltalk "image" file.

In one sense, these technical problems are of no great consequence,
because these are the standard glitches that arise with complex
software that is in the alpha stage of development. The system
overall looks to be relatively complete, full-featured and
well-designed. The time frames given by Momenta's programmers, while
optimistic, are within the bounds of plausibility.

What *is* important is the wide gap between the reality of the
current system and the appearance being promoted at Momenta's
product launch event.  A demonstration of the Momenta system was
given during that portion of the event conducted by John Rizzo,
Momenta's VP of Marketing.  A young woman holding a Momenta
computer put the system through its paces while Rizzo provided
the narrative voice-over.  The demonstration proceeded at
breakneck speed from one operation to another, providing a
user's view of the major aspects of the system, with little or
no pause between segments. 

Two video displays provided the audience with a view of the
demonstration.  A standard-size TV monitor showed an overhead
view of the woman holding the computer and moving the pen across
the surface.  In this view, the non-backlit LCD display was
dimly visible to the audience.  A second view was provided by a
wall-size projection screen which, presumably, showed the actual
contents of the screen display on the woman's computer. 

There were two disconcerting aspects about this demonstration.
First, the projection screen appeared to show the application
program's opening screen even while the presenter's computer was
still booting up (this may have been a static image projected on
the screen to fill up the time while the computer was being started).

Second, during the demo, it appeared that events shown on the large
projection screen did not quite synchronize with those shown on
actual LCD display (although this was difficult to determine,
given the small size and faint image of the first TV monitor). 
Rizzo did not actually state that the sequence shown on the
wall-size screen was what was being displayed on the presenter's
computer.  It was left up to the audience to make that
assumption, and presumably most everyone did so. In fact this
may have been the case. 

But in attempting to reconcile the speedy, flawless demo shown during
the product launch with the halting, sluggish performance shown at
OOPSLA two days later, one is has to consider the possibility that
the image shown on the projection screen during the product launch
was in fact a prepared videotape been played back to a trusting
audience.  This kind of behavior fits in with Elahian's bald
statement to the audience about shipping product today when in fact
this may not happen until early next year.  It also fits in with the
company culture, as exemplified by statements such as the quote from
Momenta CFO Albert "Rocky" Pimentel (Business Week, 10/14/91): "I'm
one of the best street fighters and mud wrestlers out there."

According to CEO Kamran Elihian, the major motivating
slogan for the company during its startup phase is: "One Thousand
Days to Immortality", and that they now have 280 days left to
reach that goal.  If Momenta persists in its questionable
PR practices, they will succeed in gaining not immortality,
but notoriety.

======================================================END========






                                                         
                                                        





